          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I am 52 and my father had a heart attack at 55.",
          "answer": "**If you are having chest pain or pressure, pain spreading to your arm, jaw or back, shortness of breath, sweating, or feeling faint, call emergency services now. Do not drive yourself.** The search tool returned an emergency instruction for your message, so I am placing it first. Your message does not describe any current symptoms, so this may not apply to you, but if anything like this is happening, act now.\n\nThe guidance I could retrieve does not directly address screening for people whose father had a heart attack at a young age. None of the retrieved passages mention family history of heart disease as a risk factor.\n\nThe closest passage is from the lipid screening guidance (major cardiology society consensus). It says a cholesterol panel is the usual screening test, that routine screening is every four to six years for low-risk adults aged 20 to 75, and that screening should be more frequent where risk factors are present. It also says treatment decisions depend on estimated 10-year heart and blood vessel risk, not on the cholesterol number alone. This guidance has a section marked as disagreement, which I could not review in full here.\n\nThe other retrieved documents covered reflux, anxiety, migraine, and established heart disease after a diagnosis, so they do not apply to your question.\n\nBecause your father's heart attack was at 55, it is reasonable to ask your doctor whether your family history changes how often you should be checked and what your 10-year risk is. Bring your father's age at diagnosis to that conversation.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
