          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Does that change what I should be screened for?",
          "answer": "The retrieved guidance suggests family history can matter, but it does not say which screening tests you should have.\n\nThe lipid guidance (major cardiology society consensus) lists family history of premature cardiovascular disease as a \"risk-enhancing feature.\" It says these features can shift a decision when a person's estimated 10-year risk falls in the borderline (5 to below 7.5 percent) or intermediate (7.5 to below 20 percent) range. It also says the 10-year risk estimate, not the cholesterol number alone, drives treatment decisions. The passage I received does not define what counts as \"premature,\" so I cannot say whether your father's age at diagnosis meets that definition. This document also has a disagreement section that I could not review.\n\nThe diabetes screening guidance (national diabetes association standards of care) says adults are screened from age 35. Your age falls within that recommendation whatever your family history. Its listed extra risk factors include a first-degree relative with diabetes, but your father's heart attack is not diabetes, so that passage does not apply to your situation.\n\nThe other retrieved documents (reflux, anxiety) do not address this question.\n\nYour doctor can work out your 10-year risk using your age, blood pressure, cholesterol, smoking status, and other factors, and can judge whether your family history changes that estimate. Bring your father's age at his heart attack to that appointment.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
