          "type": "turn",
          "question": "From what age?",
          "answer": "The retrieved guidance does not give a starting age for screening based on family history. The search also returned a note saying part of the result was removed, so I may be missing material.\n\nThe ages I did find are these:\n\n- **Lipid screening (major cardiology society consensus):** The usual interval is every four to six years for adults aged 20 to 75 at low risk. The passage says screening should be more often where risk factors are present, but it does not list family history as a trigger for a particular starting age.\n- **Diabetes screening (national diabetes association standards of care):** Adults are screened from age 35, regardless of family history.\n- **Diabetes screening (preventive-services guidance):** This guidance restricts routine screening to adults aged 35 to 70 who are overweight or obese. It disagrees with the diabetes association's position, and the passage does not say how family history fits in.\n\nAt 52, you fall within the age ranges in both the lipid and diabetes passages. Those ranges do not address your father's heart attack at 55.\n\nYour doctor can tell you which tests fit your situation and how often to have them. Bring your father's age at diagnosis.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
